                    briefing_text_r2:       `Select up to four levers from a costed menu, across at least two of the three families. <strong>Structural:</strong> a single account-owner role + redrawn reporting lines ($250k). <strong>Process:</strong> redesign the order-to-resolution workflow ($300k), reconfigure the ERP ($450k). <strong>People:</strong> capability training ($360k), leadership coaching ($180k), recognition + role clarity ($120k). Most real gaps are multi-causal — a single-lever fix rarely closes them, and the comfortable people-only default leaves a broken structure and process untouched. Match your mix to the root cause you diagnosed.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Sequence the change plan under the budget cap`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The constraint bites: <strong>$1.5M and six months</strong> fund two levers fully plus one light-touch. Sequencing matters — fixing a process while roles are still ambiguous fails, and training people on a process you're about to redesign wastes the spend. Structural clarity usually comes first, then process, then capability, with the <strong>change-management wrap</strong> running throughout. Define leading indicators and named owners, then absorb the <em>mid-plan shock</em>: a key supervisor resigns and the ERP vendor raises its quote. Two levers done well and sequenced beat five run at once in a unit already destabilised by two reorgs.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover, measure & defend to the CEO`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present your recovery case to the CEO. A technically correct redesign that nobody adopts produces <em>zero</em> performance lift — and this unit is at 51% engagement with two failed reorganisations behind it, so adoption is everything. Show how you drive genuine buy-in, what <strong>refreezes</strong> the gain into job descriptions, the ERP and incentives, and whether your projected trajectory is real adoption or a paper number. The CEO probes the classic OD failures: a fix that ignores the root cause, and a correct design that no one adopts. Defend every dollar against the $60M of retained revenue.`,

                    r1_root_label:          `Across structural, process and people, where is the primary root cause?`,
                    r1_reinforcing_label:   `Which reinforcing cause sits directly beneath the 6.8% order-error rate?`,
                    r1_rigor_label:         `How rigorously does your team trace evidence to root cause before naming a fix? (%)`,
