                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_root_right:    `<strong>That's the right primary root cause.</strong> Two reorganisations left overlapping roles and no single account owner — that's structural, and it's the soil every other symptom grows in. Now you can fix what's actually broken instead of retraining people to do a job no one is accountable for.`,
                    coach_r1_process:       `<strong>Process is genuinely part of the gap</strong> — the order-to-resolution flow is broken and the ERP is misconfigured. But fix the flow while roles are still ambiguous and the new workflow inherits the same orphaned hand-offs. What sits beneath the broken process?`,
                    coach_r1_people:        `<strong>Careful — that's the comfortable HR diagnosis.</strong> You saw a 6.8% error rate and reached for training. But the errors cluster in returns, where two teams both think the other owns the hand-off — that's structural, not a training gap. You'd be spending to train people to do a job no one is accountable for.`,
                    coach_r1_individuals:   `<strong>Blaming individuals is the easy answer and the wrong one.</strong> When a 240-person unit is missing on errors, response and resolution after two reorgs and an ERP rollout, that's a system and structure problem, not careless people. Diagnose the roles, the flow and the system.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the primary cause as structural AND traced the error rate to the orphaned returns hand-off where no one owns the work. That is exactly the lens that lets you pick the right lever first instead of the most visible one.`,
                    coach_r1_skills:        `<strong>That's symptom-chasing.</strong> Retraining all 240 treats the visible metric and ignores the ownerless hand-off underneath it. You'd spend $360k teaching people to do a job no role is accountable for — and the errors return.`,
                    coach_r1_symptom:       `<strong>The error rate is a symptom, not the cause.</strong> 'Fix it directly' is exactly how you order ERP retraining for a structural problem. There is a deeper cause — and it lives in who owns the returns hand-off, not in the headline number.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigor:      `<strong>This is how the most expensive OD error starts.</strong> Jumping to a favourite fix before tracing evidence to root cause means you'll spend the budget on the wrong lever. Diagnose before you intervene.`,
                    coach_r2_mix_strong:    `<strong>A structure-plus-process backbone.</strong> Creating a single account owner gives the redesigned flow someone to own it — structure and process reinforcing each other. That's the multi-lever logic a multi-cause gap demands.`,
                    coach_r2_mix_full:      `<strong>A mix across all three families.</strong> Structure to own it, process to fix the flow, and a people lever to run it — well chosen. Just watch the budget and the unit's capacity to absorb change; two done well beat everything half-done.`,
                    coach_r2_mix_partial:   `<strong>You've spanned two families — good instinct.</strong> But check whether you've actually addressed the structural root: without a single account owner, even a redesigned process inherits the orphaned hand-off. Make sure the structural lever is in the mix.`,
                    coach_r2_peopleonly:    `<strong>Your plan is all training, coaching and recognition.</strong> Important — but the order-to-resolution process is broken and the ERP is misconfigured. You can motivate people brilliantly and they'll still fail inside a broken system. Where's your structural or process lever?`,
                    coach_r2_singlelever:   `<strong>One lever won't cascade across a multi-cause gap.</strong> Structure, process and people are all contributing here; fix one and the others keep dragging the KPIs down. The whole point of the diagnosis was that no single fix closes it.`,
                    coach_r2_visible:       `<strong>Chasing the visible metric is the classic trap.</strong> Pick levers by what moves the error rate fastest and you'll buy a short-lived dip, not a closed gap. Choose levers by root cause, not by which symptom is loudest.`,
                    coach_r3_structurefirst:`<strong>Structure first is the right sequence.</strong> Clarify who owns the account and the hand-offs, then redesign the process onto that clarity, then build capability. The new workflow finally has an owner — that's how you avoid inheriting the old orphaned hand-offs.`,
                    coach_r3_processfirst:  `<strong>You've designed a beautiful new workflow with no one to own it.</strong> The same role ambiguity that broke the old process breaks the new one — watch adoption, it has the same orphaned hand-offs. Which has to come first, the structure or the process?`,
                    coach_r3_peoplefirst:   `<strong>Training people on a process you're about to redesign wastes the spend.</strong> They learn the old way, you change it, and you pay to retrain. Sequence the structure and process first, then build capability onto the design that will actually stick.`,
                    coach_r3_allatonce:     `<strong>Five changes at once in an already-destabilised unit.</strong> The unit can't absorb it — change fatigue will sink adoption after two reorgs and an ERP rollout. Two levers done well and sequenced beat everything launched simultaneously.`,
                    coach_r3_nochange:      `<strong>Almost nothing for the change wrap.</strong> A technically correct plan with no communication, involvement or adoption support rolls out to silence and resistance. Buy-in isn't a soft extra — it's what converts a designed change into a realised one.`,
                    coach_r3_disciplined:   `<strong>Disciplined sequencing.</strong> Structure first, a funded change wrap, and leading indicators with named owners per lever — that's a plan that lands in order and that you can steer mid-flight instead of judging only at the end.`,
                    coach_r3_noindicators:  `<strong>No leading indicators, no named owners.</strong> You'll only find out the plan slipped at the end, when it's too late to correct — and with no owner per lever, accountability evaporates. Define what you watch and who owns it before you launch.`,
                    coach_r3_addlever:      `<strong>Adding a fifth lever under stress makes it worse.</strong> The unit is already at capacity; bolting on an emergency intervention deepens the change fatigue and risks blowing the budget. Hold the sequence and absorb the shock from the light-touch lever.`,
                    coach_r3_freeze:        `<strong>Freezing the whole plan stalls the momentum you've built.</strong> The three at-risk accounts renew within six months — a full freeze burns the time you don't have. Promote a named internal owner and keep the sequence moving.`,
                    coach_r4_involve:       `<strong>Adoption, not design quality alone, drives results.</strong> Involving the unit in designing the change, communicating the why and coaching the new owners is how a 51%-engagement unit comes to own a redesign instead of resisting it.`,
                    coach_r4_mandate:       `<strong>Mandate-and-audit buys a number, not buy-in.</strong> In a unit that's lost trust after two reorgs, ordering compliance produces paper numbers that collapse the moment the audit pressure lifts. A correct design at low adoption produces almost no real lift.`,
                    coach_r4_email:         `<strong>An email and a town-hall is not an adoption plan.</strong> You rolled a redesign out to a low-trust, low-engagement unit and walked away. Adoption tracks at 30%, and a correct design at 30% adoption produces almost no performance lift. Who did you involve in designing it?`,
                    coach_r4_momentum:      `<strong>'Momentum will carry it' is how the gain decays.</strong> Without embedding the new roles into job descriptions, the ERP and the incentives, the old behaviours return by Q4 and CSAT slides back. What refreezes this so it survives after your project ends?`,
                    coach_r4_repeat:        `<strong>Relaunching every quarter isn't sustainment.</strong> If the gain needs a fresh push to survive, it was never refrozen. Embed it into the systems and incentives so it holds without you driving it.`,
                    coach_r4_honest:        `<strong>That's a CEO-ready, honest case.</strong> Real buy-in rising on leading indicators, the gain embedded into roles and incentives, and every dollar defended against the $60M of retained revenue — that's a trajectory that survives, not a number that collapses.`,
                    coach_r4_coerce:        `<strong>You've coerced the metrics to target.</strong> The dashboard looks good for the CEO, but staff are hitting the number without owning the change — and it collapses the week the pressure lifts. Is that real recovery, or a paper trajectory?`,
                    coach_r4_declare:       `<strong>A Q1 error-rate dip is not a closed gap.</strong> The last initiative spiked then decayed the moment attention moved on. Declaring victory on an early dip with no sustainment plan is exactly how this unit got here. What holds the gain through Q4?`,
                    coach_overbudget:       `<strong>You've blown the $1.5M cap.</strong> Funding too many levers at once leaves nothing for the change wrap and overloads a unit already destabilised by two reorgs. The budget was a hard constraint — two strong levers plus one light-touch, not five.`,

