                    insight_embedded:       `Gap closed and refrozen: you turned a contested multi-cause diagnosis into a sequenced, adoption-led recovery — service recovery on a credible trajectory backed by genuine buy-in and embedded into roles, the ERP and incentives. You fixed the root cause, not the symptom.`,
                    insight_paper:          `Paper recovery: the headline hit the target, but adoption lagged badly. Staff are hitting the number without owning the change — this is the trajectory that collapses the week the audit pressure lifts, and the at-risk accounts won't be fooled for long.`,
                    insight_thin:           `You moved the number but buy-in is thin. The CEO may be satisfied at end of Q2, yet without genuine adoption and a refreeze the gain is fragile and likely to decay back toward baseline.`,
                    insight_short:          `You fell short of the credible ≥70 recovery trajectory. The CEO loses patience, the three at-risk accounts churn, and a top-down restructuring is imposed over your head. Re-examine which family you under-weighted — most likely the structural root and the sequence.`,
                    insight_decay:          `Sustainability is low — the plan looks like a project, not a changed organisation. Without embedded roles, named owners and live indicators, the improvement slides back to baseline once your project ends.`,
                    insight_lowadoption:    `Adoption stayed low: staff complied without owning the change. Genuine adoption comes from involving the unit, sequencing sensibly and a funded change wrap — not from mandate-and-audit.`,
                    insight_overbudget:     `You overspent the $1.5M cap. Spreading the budget across too many simultaneous levers starved the change wrap and overloaded a unit that two reorgs had already destabilised.`,
                    insight_stretch:        `You reached the peer benchmark — exceptional, but only meaningful if the adoption and sustainability behind it are real and refrozen.`,
                    insight_lasts:          `This is change that lasts: recovery, genuine adoption and sustainability moving together. Six months after your project ends, the new roles and process are still in use and the accounts have renewed.`,

                    hint_paper:             `Recovery is rising faster than real adoption — you're building a paper trajectory. Shift from mandate-and-audit to involvement, a funded change wrap and a sequence that earns buy-in.`,
                    hint_sustain:           `Sustainability is dangerously low. Define leading indicators, name an owner per lever, and embed the new roles into job descriptions and incentives, or this decays once your project ends.`,
                    hint_adoption:          `Adoption is weak — staff aren't owning the change. Reconsider your sequence and the change-management wrap; involvement and clear ownership move practice more than a mandate.`,
                    hint_behind:            `You're behind the credible ≥70 trajectory with little time left. Concentrate on the structural root cause and a sequence that puts ownership before process.`,
                    hint_overbudget:        `You've exceeded the $1.5M cap. Drop a lever and protect the change-management wrap — two strong interventions plus one light-touch beat five run at once.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — recovery, adoption and sustainability all healthy. Now lock in named owners, leading indicators and the refreeze so it survives the handover.`,

